Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Great upper body gains this week, but your legs urgently need more attention.",
  "summary": "This was a productive week for muscle building with solid strength gains across your upper body. Chest and back both made meaningful jumps in their strength index, and shoulders and triceps continued their steady climb. Overall tonnage and volume are respectable for your profile as a 28-year-old male at a healthy BMI of 23.5. The one clear issue pulling things back is the severe under-training of legs, which received just a single set this week and showed zero strength progress. Keeping this imbalance going will limit your long-term muscle-building results and could create structural problems down the line. The good news is that everything else is trending in the right direction, so a targeted adjustment is all that's needed.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "on_track",
    "notes": "Progressive overload is on track for most muscle groups. Chest improved from 104 to 108 and back from 104 to 107, both solid single-period gains. Shoulders ticked up from 102 to 103 and triceps from 103 to 104, showing steady incremental progress. Biceps held flat at 101, which is not alarming over one period but worth watching. Legs remained completely stagnant at 100, which is directly tied to only 1 set being logged — there is simply not enough stimulus for any adaptation to occur there."
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Legs",
      "severity": "severe",
      "observation": "Legs received only 1 set this week, accounting for just 4.6% of total volume. The strength index shows zero progress at 100, consistent with chronic under-stimulation. For a muscle-building goal, legs typically require 10 to 20 sets per week to drive hypertrophy."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps volume at 4.9% of total and only 3 sets is on the low end, and the strength index flatlined this period. As an isolation muscle that supports both back training and overall arm development, a small volume increase could break the stall."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Add at least 4 to 6 sets of compound leg work next period, such as squats, Romanian deadlifts, or leg press, to begin closing the severe leg volume gap and restart strength progress there.",
    "Maintain the current chest and back volume and loading since both are progressing well, but continue adding small increments of weight or reps to sustain the upward trend in their strength indexes.",
    "Add one extra set of biceps work to nudge that strength index off its plateau, targeting progressive overload with slightly heavier loads or an extra rep rather than just more volume.",
    "Audit your weekly schedule to ensure legs are placed in a dedicated slot rather than being treated as optional, since consistent leg training is essential for balanced muscle growth across your whole body."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Strong upper body strength gains this period with chest and back leading the way, but legs received only 1 set and showed zero progress, making leg volume the single most urgent priority for next period."
}
